{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. Here's the full picture:\n\n## Why you never got a single message\n\nThe launchd job itself was healthy — `runs = 6`, and its stderr log captured a failure every night. The failure was entirely inside `bin/nightly-review.zsh`, where two bugs compound:\n\n**1. The silence: `status` is read-only in zsh (the actual cause of zero notifications)**\n`bin/nightly-review.zsh:19` runs `status=$?`. In zsh, `status` is a read-only special variable (an alias for `$?`), so the assignment fails and aborts the script on that line — before the `if` block that calls `notify.sh`. It's fatal *unconditionally*, so neither the \"done\" nor the \"FAILED\" branch could ever run, even if `claude` had succeeded. Your `nightly-review.err` shows `read-only variable: status` on all 6 runs, matching `last exit code = 1`. I reproduced this exactly with stub `claude`/`notify.sh` before touching anything.\n\n**2. Why it failed every night: `claude` isn't logged in under launchd**\nEach run's stderr also shows `Not logged in · Please run /login` — the launchd-spawned session has no usable credentials even though your interactive shell is logged in. This part can't be fixed from the snapshot; it needs an interactive `claude` login on the real machine, ideally followed by one manual `launchctl kickstart gui/501/com.vk.nightly-review` to confirm.\n\n## What I changed\n\n- `bin/nightly-review.zsh` — renamed `status` → `rc`, and added `|| echo \"nightly-review: notify.sh failed\" >&2` to all three `notify.sh` calls so a failing notifier leaves evidence in `nightly-review.err` instead of disappearing quietly.\n- `ANSWER.md` — full write-up of both causes with the evidence.\n- Verified with a stub harness: failure path now sends \"nightly review FAILED (exit 1)…\", success path sends \"nightly review done: …\". Scratch files cleaned up; only the script edit and `ANSWER.md` are new (see `git status`).\n\nOne minor note in the answer file too: the `> \"$OUT\"` redirection creates an empty `nightly-review-<date>.md` even on failed nights — that's why those empty files exist in `~/Library/Logs`."}
